  <!-- Educational Content Slot (Pure English SEO-Optimized) -->
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What is Body Mass Index (BMI)?</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Body Mass Index (BMI)</strong> is an internationally recognized screening
      metric established by the World Health Organization (WHO) to assess whether
      an individual has a healthy body weight for their height. It is widely used
      by healthcare professionals, dietitians, and fitness experts to classify individuals
      into <strong>underweight</strong>, <strong>normal weight</strong>, <strong
        >overweight</strong
      >, and <strong>obese</strong> categories.
    </p>

    <!-- Mathematical Formula -->
    <h3>1. BMI Formula for Men & Women</h3>
    <p>
      The mathematical formula to calculate BMI is uniform for all adult men and
      women:
    </p>

    <div
      class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100/80 p-4 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900"
    >
      <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">
        Metric Formula (kg & cm):
      </div>
      <div class="mt-1">BMI = Weight (kg) / [Height (meters)]²</div>

      <div class="mt-3 font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">
        US / Imperial Formula (lbs & inches):
      </div>
      <div class="mt-1">BMI = 703 × Weight (lbs) / [Height (inches)]²</div>
    </div>

    <h4>Example Calculation:</h4>
    <p>
      If a person weighs <strong>70 kg</strong> and has a height of <strong
        >1.75 meters</strong
      > (5 feet 9 inches):
    </p>
    <div
      class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-3 text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono dark:bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800"
    >
      BMI = 70 / (1.75 × 1.75) = 70 / 3.0625 ≈ <strong>22.86 kg/m²</strong> (Healthy
      / Normal Weight)
    </div>

    <!-- WHO vs Asian Standards Table -->
    <h3>2. WHO vs Asian/Indian BMI Classification Standards</h3>
    <p>
      While the WHO Global Standard applies broadly across worldwide
      populations, epidemiological research has revealed that Asian and Indian
      populations carry a higher percentage of visceral body fat at lower BMI
      values. The World Health Organization and the Ministry of Health and
      Family Welfare (MOHFW) recommend the Asian-specific classification:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-4">
      <table class="min-w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr
            class="border-b border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900"
          >
            <th class="py-3 px-3 font-bold">Category</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-3 font-bold">WHO Global BMI Range</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-3 font-bold">Asian / Indian BMI Range</th>
            <th class="py-3 px-3 font-bold">Associated Health Risk</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 dark:divide-slate-800">
          <tr>
            <td
              class="py-2.5 px-3 font-semibold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400"
              >Underweight</td
            >
            <td class="py-2.5 px-3">&lt; 18.5</td>
            <td class="py-2.5 px-3">&lt; 18.5</td>
            <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Nutritional deficiency, lower immunity</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td
              class="py-2.5 px-3 font-semibold text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400"
